---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import ContentSection from '../../components/ContentSection.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import LinkCard from '../../components/LinkCard.astro';

const relatedPages = [
  { title: 'NBA Betting Hub', href: '/nba-betting', description: 'Complete NBA guide' },
  { title: 'NBA Playoffs', href: '/nba/playoff-betting', description: 'Postseason wagering' },
  { title: 'Common Mistakes', href: '/common-betting-mistakes', description: 'Avoiding errors' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Load Management & Scheduling Effects"
  description="How NBA rest patterns, back-to-backs, and load management affect betting lines and outcomes throughout the regular season."
>
  <Hero
    title="Load Management & Scheduling Effects"
    subtitle="Rest, Fatigue, and Their Impact on NBA Betting"
    size="medium"
  />

  <div class="container content-container">
    <article class="page-content">
      <ContentSection>
        <div class="quick-take">
          <h2>Quick Take</h2>
          <p>
            NBA teams strategically rest star players throughout the 82-game regular season. Back-to-backs, travel, and long road trips all affect performance. These scheduling factors are priced into betting lines, but understanding how they work helps you evaluate whether the market adjustment is appropriate. Rest matters—but the market knows it matters.
          </p>
        </div>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What Is Load Management?" id="definition">
        <p>
          Load management refers to teams resting healthy players to prevent injury and preserve performance for the playoffs. Star players regularly miss games, especially on back-to-backs, during long road trips, or late in the season when seeding is determined.
        </p>
        <p>
          This creates significant betting implications because the team you analyzed might not field the lineup you expected.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="How Does Scheduling Affect Performance?" id="scheduling">
        <h3>Back-to-backs</h3>
        <p>
          Teams playing their second game in two nights typically perform worse. Star players may rest, and those who play show decreased efficiency. The market adjusts lines accordingly—typically 1-3 points.
        </p>

        <h3>Road trip length</h3>
        <p>
          Extended road trips (4+ games) wear teams down progressively. The fourth game of a road trip is different from the first.
        </p>

        <h3>Travel distance</h3>
        <p>
          A team flying from Miami to Portland faces more fatigue than one traveling from LA to Sacramento. Time zone changes matter, especially for early tip-offs.
        </p>

        <h3>Rest advantages</h3>
        <p>
          Teams coming off multiple rest days face teams on back-to-backs frequently. This rest differential creates predictable line movements.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What This Means for Bettors" id="what-means">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Check injury reports close to game time:</strong> Load management decisions are often announced late.</li>
          <li><strong>Understand the market adjusts:</strong> Rest effects are priced in. The question is whether the adjustment is accurate.</li>
          <li><strong>Consider totals:</strong> Tired teams may play slower or less effectively on both ends.</li>
          <li><strong>Track team-specific patterns:</strong> Some teams manage load aggressively; others play through fatigue.</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Common Misconceptions" id="misconceptions">
        <h3>"Always fade teams on back-to-backs"</h3>
        <p>
          The market knows about back-to-backs. Lines already account for fatigue. Blindly betting against tired teams isn't profitable.
        </p>

        <h3>"Rest always helps"</h3>
        <p>
          Too much rest can lead to rust. Teams off extended breaks sometimes start slowly.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Key Takeaways" id="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>Load management is common for star players, especially on back-to-backs</li>
          <li>Scheduling factors are priced into lines—the market knows</li>
          <li>Check injury reports close to game time for late scratches</li>
          <li>Evaluate whether market adjustments are appropriate, not just whether factors exist</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Related Resources" id="related">
        <div class="related-grid">
          {relatedPages.map((page) => (
            <LinkCard title={page.title} href={page.href} description={page.description} />
          ))}
        </div>
      </ContentSection>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-content { padding: var(--spacing-3xl) 0; }
  .quick-take { background-color: var(--color-surface); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent); padding: var(--spacing-lg); border-radius: 0 var(--border-radius) var(--border-radius) 0; margin-bottom: var(--spacing-xl); }
  .quick-take h2 { font-size: 1rem; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.05em; color: var(--color-accent); margin-bottom: var(--spacing-sm); }
  .quick-take p { margin: 0; color: var(--color-text-secondary); }
  .related-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: var(--spacing-md); margin-top: var(--spacing-lg); }
  @media (max-width: 768px) { .related-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>
